<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Castries</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Castries is the capital and main port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a>. Originally called Le Carénage for its sheltered harbour, it was renamed in 1785 after the French naval minister, the Marquis de Castries. The harbour made Saint Lucia strategically valuable, and the island changed hands between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> many times before British rule became permanent in 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castries.jpg" alt="Castries" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Castries_Saint_Lucia_Day248bdriveb.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Castries stands on a flood plain and is built on reclaimed land, laid out in a grid. Its sheltered, deep-water harbour gave the settlement its first name, Carénage, a French word meaning safe anchorage. The harbour receives cargo vessels, ferries and cruise ships. Morne Fortune, a hill of 258 metres, rises above the city and carries Fort Charlotte.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1650 a group of 40 Frenchmen led by de Rousselan founded a fort near the Petit Cul-de-Sac and the Carénage river, when the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a> had been purchased from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-west-india-company">French West India Company</a> by du Parquet and Houel. Governor Baron de Micoud moved the capital to the south side of the harbour in 1769. The village of Carénage was renamed Castries after Charles Eugène Gabriel de La Croix, marquis de Castries, the French Minister of the Navy and Colonies; his title comes from a commune in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>Castries has been rebuilt many times after major fires, on 15 October 1805, on 6 April 1813 and, most severely, on 19 June 1948. Under British rule a wharf was built to serve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> trade, and the first steamship, the RMS Solway, arrived in 1841. On 9 March 1942 the German submarine U-161 entered the harbour at night and sank two Allied ships, among them the Canadian ocean liner RMS Lady Nelson, which was refloated and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> to be converted into a hospital ship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Castries is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a> and the seat of government, with an urban population of about 20,000 and a district of just under 70,000. Landmarks include the Cathedral of the Immaculate Conception, the Government House and Derek Walcott Square, renamed from Columbus Square for the island's Nobel Prize-winning poet, who was born in the city, as was the economist Arthur Lewis. The city also hosts the secretariat of the Organisation of Eastern Caribbean States and the headquarters of the Eastern Caribbean Supreme Court.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Castries” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The British kept much of the French legal system and Catholic church organisation that they found, and <b>Castries</b> remained the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-lucia">Colony of Saint Lucia</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fine harbour, <b>Le Carénage</b>, renamed Castries in 1785 after the naval minister, made it strategically valuable, and its slopes were planted with sugar, coffee and cacao by settlers and enslaved Africans, mostly after 1763.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-saint-lucia">French Saint Lucia</a></li><li><blockquote>Derek Walcott, born in <b>Castries</b>, won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1992, after Arthur Lewis's economics prize in 1979.</blockquote><a href="/state/saint-lucia">Saint Lucia</a></li></ul></section>
